Veloso wrote:
> Here's an idea I've been toying around with on my local copy of
> Nethack, and I wanted to ask for opinions.
>
> At the start of a game, one of the nine glass colors is randomly
> selected to be the color for a new, rare sort of gemstone, a chameleon
> stone.  When #rubbed against any other item, it turns into a (possibly
> inferior) copy of that item.
>
> Stacks are significantly reduced in size (average new-stack size is
> 25%).  Artifacts clone into their non-artifact form, wands of wishing
> and magic lamps clone into wands of nothing and oil lamps, and
> statues/tins/corpses of unique monsters clone into statues/tins/corpses
> of chameleons.  The clone object might be inferior, that is, with a
> lower bonus, fewer charges, missing fooproofing, possibly eroded, etc.
> The B/C/U status of the stone affects the odds of an inferior copy.
>
> Chameleon stones don't stack, and the cloned item takes the inventory
> slot of the chameleon stone, once used.
>
> They are very rare (.5% of all gems) and can occur on any dungeon
> level.  Chameleons also have a small chance (affected by luck) of
> leaving one behind when killed.
>
> Good uses that I can think of are cloning a magic marker or wand of
> death, creating a clone of a powerful weapon for two-hand use, and
> cloning a large stack of gold before buying protection.  I'm sure rgrn
> readers can offer others.
>
> Here's what I like about the idea:
> * It makes gems more interesting, and their identification a bit
> trickier.  Using a touchstone is now a bit risky, as a chameleon stone
> so tested would turn into a second touchstone.  Likewise, testing
> hardness by engraving makes a chameleon stone become a piece of the
> floor.
> * It's a nifty wish-light, which still has good uses but is not quite
> so powerful.  It is a viable candidate as a reward at an end of a new
> branch.
> * I find it interesting that, if you intend clone an item with charges,
> it pays to do it early.  For example, if you find an early magic
> marker, you may be tempted to hold off on using it until you find a
> chameleon stone to duplicate it with.  (This is probably only a factor
> if there is to be a guaranteed chameleon stone somewhere.)
> * Makes a wishless game a tad easier (though this might fairly be
> considered a downside).
>
> Because these are clearly powerful, compensating action must be taken.
> Slightly reducing the probability of magic lamps is a possibility, as
> is increasing their cost significantly.
>
> I'm interested in all opinions, and I am specifically wondering if you
> see any game-breaking uses of chameleon stones that I missed.
>
> Greg F

I'm not sure about the 'engraving' part, because that isn't rubbing.
And if you're going to count any kind of prolonged touch, merely
holding the chameleon stone should turn it into a disembodied hand, no?
Or into a backpack as it tosses around inside.



